From b5d4bf65930be0f0dcfdede7a2599aa1658e2356 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 15:19:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: send Dolby Vision as HDR10 where it cannot be sent
 natively

The base layer of a Dolby Vision stream of profile 5 is not a picture
by itself: it is coded in IPTPQc2, which only the reshaping and the
matrices of the RPU turn into colours. Kodi plays it as it is wherever
native output is not to be had (a display without Dolby Vision
Standard, a TV input in its game mode, a view that is zoomed), and the
colours are wrong: green and purple. The base layer of the other
streams is the version for displays without Dolby Vision, and plays as
HDR10, HLG or SDR; the one exception by the profiles is AV1 of profile
10 whose record says that its base layer is compatible with nothing
(bl_signal_compatibility_id 0), which is taken like profile 5
(DVBRIDGE::BaseLayerIsNoPicture(), which also says for which streams a
picture that comes with the RPU of an earlier one is drawn with it). No
such stream was at hand; that the metadata of an AV1 picture counts as
its own is the commit before the engine's.

Such a stream is now reconstructed and sent as HDR10 where the display
takes HDR10 and the picture is not drawn natively. The reconstruction
is the one of native output (the engine of the commit before this
one): what the display gets is the Dolby Vision picture in BT.2020 PQ,
with the static metadata that its RPU gives, and none of the stream's
own where the RPU gives none (the light levels and the mastering
luminance of the stream are those of its base layer). It is not tone
mapped here; the display maps it as it maps any HDR10.

It is a way of drawing the video inside Kodi's own HDR10 output, not
another output: the renderer is configured with a picture that
describes what is sent (PQ, BT.2020, 10 bits, the mastering luminance
and the light levels of the RPU), and from there on the output mode,
the HDR signalling, the output depth (also the floating-point output of
NVIDIA), the GUI over the video and the return to SDR are Kodi's. In
place of Kodi's shader, the picture is reconstructed at the place and
size Kodi gives it and copied into the window: any window size, any
view mode, zoom and shift; the limited range when Kodi's output is set
to it.

- The decoder keeps the Dolby Vision metadata and, for profile 7, the
  enhancement layer of a stream that is reconstructed, as it does for
  native output, and marks its pictures (VideoPicture::doviConvert).
  Only where the picture can be sent as HDR10: on an output that does
  not take it (an SDR display, or HDR turned off in the settings) the
  stream goes to the decoder it went to before, which decodes it as
  before.
- CLinuxRendererGLES asks DVBRIDGE::WantsConversion() for a picture
  that it does not draw natively, the picture first: a video that is
  not Dolby Vision does not come to the question of native output,
  which it never came to. CRendererVAAPIGLES gives the layer and the
  two rectangles (decoder textures only, as for native output, of
  3840x2160 at most, with no limit on the geometry).
- The window system reconstructs with a bridge of its own
  (CDVBridgeGLES::PrepareConversion(), CopyConversion()), apart from
  the one of native output. The GL state that Kodi goes on drawing with
  is put back after the set-up of that bridge and after each
  reconstruction (dvbridge_gl_state of the engine).
- Kodi draws its frame at every refresh, also while it is paused: a
  picture that comes again for the same place is not reconstructed
  again, only copied (each picture that comes into a buffer of the
  renderer has a serial). On the integrated GPU a reconstruction with
  FEL takes most of a refresh. One that could not be reconstructed is
  tried again.

In an output mode in which native output is to be had, nothing
changes: a picture that would be drawn natively if it had its metadata
(the first pictures of a stream may come without) is not sent as HDR10
on the way to it. A video that starts in another mode (in a window, or
with a GUI that is not 3840x2160) is reconstructed and sent as HDR10
until the mode for native output comes; before this, a profile 5 video
showed its base layer until then.

Fail closed. When the output does not take HDR10, the renderer is
configured as Kodi configures it. A picture that cannot be
reconstructed shows the one before it when nothing of it was drawn (its
metadata does not hold, a layer cannot be imported). When there is none
before it, or the reconstruction failed halfway (the texture then holds
no whole picture), that frame has no video. After 48 such pictures in a
row the video plays as Kodi plays it, until the next video: with a
failure that every picture meets, that is two seconds without video,
and a second texture to keep the last whole picture in (132 MB at
3840x2160) would only show one still picture for that long. A stream
whose RPUs stay missing goes back to Kodi's path
by the rule that native output has. Whether the display takes HDR10 is
what Kodi's own HDR output goes by (read from the display when Kodi
starts, and the setting), as it is when the video starts.

Two settings, in the settings of the display, shown when the display
is an HDR one:
- "Dolby Vision as HDR10 when needed", on: profile 5.
- "Also with an enhancement layer", off: profile 7 with its enhancement
  layer is reconstructed too, instead of playing its HDR10 base layer.
  What is sent is then the Dolby Vision master with the FEL residual,
  mapped by the display as plain HDR10. It is not the default: the base
  layer of such a stream is a graded HDR10 version, and which of the
  two looks better on a display is for the user to judge.

The player information says "Dolby Vision rebuilt, sent as HDR10", and
which layers went into the picture.

The rule and the geometry are tested on the host
(tests/dvconvert_test.cpp: which streams, and where the picture goes
for a window that is filled, bars, pillars, a smaller window, zoom,
shift, a cropped view and 200000 random pairs of rectangles). The
engine is tested by drawing as this commit uses it: a renderer made for
HDR10 alone, the window as the target, first row at the bottom, limited
range, and the GL state of a caller around it (the commit before this
one). Nothing of this has run on a GPU, and the code of this commit
has only been read and checked for syntax: whether the picture is
right on a display, and how the display maps it, is for the first test
on hardware.
